plan:   device-cache-mb 10275      [measured] the experts used most live on the processor that computes them fastest, as the RAM cache holds what flash would read: CUDA0 computes this model's experts at 251 GiB/s against the host's 25, both measured on this model's own expert block; a cache of 10275 MiB (10125 the capacity fitter gave 15 of 48 layers' experts, 150 it left free under one more whole layer) serves 93% of a token's routed bytes (from this model's own routing); a miss is uploaded from the host
plan:   ubatch         0          [derived] a graph as wide as the context reserves 110 MiB of compute buffers, inside 10% of the 30165 MiB this process may hold: a prompt is computed in one pass
plan:   placement      49 layers on devices [measured] the capacity fitter found a placement that is projected to fit; 48 of 48 MoE layers keep their experts on the host, host-resident 110 MiB, on devices 4408 MiB, context 2048 (fitter's host breakdown: model 49780, context 0, compute 110 MiB; file 63336, experts 32400)
plan:   ctx-size       2048       [policy] the value this run was given, not a choice: the capacity fitter only picks a context when it is handed a zero and this planner always pins it, so nothing has weighed this number against what the memory it reserves would buy as expert cache
plan:   regime         experts-stream [derived] on the host 32510 MiB (dense 110) against 24132 MiB usable, which is the 30165 MiB this process may hold (as reported available, unmeasured) less a 6033 MiB margin (the process is killed)
plan:   extra-offload  none       [policy] the fitter has already placed what fits on the devices - see the placement line above - and placing weights across devices is a capacity problem it solves exactly; this planner owns the tier below it and does not second-guess it
plan:   experts        host       [derived] this device could compute them on paper - it offers a host buffer and runs the model's own layout - and it still cannot. The streamer rebinds every expert onto memory it reserved itself, which no device was given access to; and a host buffer type would not survive the load anyway, since a mapped model has one substituted for the CPU's. Whether this device can wrap memory a caller already owns - the one opening left - is unprobed. What it advertises is a blanket promise, and a backend whose support is conditional declines to make one while implementing the call anyway, so the absence of a yes decides nothing here
plan:   dense-weights  anon       [derived] no reclaim-exempt store here, so anonymous buffers are what keeps the dense set off a flash refault (a reclaim then means: the process is killed)
plan:   prefill-device off        [derived] a device expert cache is set, which keeps the layers on their device and computes the experts there; a prefill device is not combined with it
plan:   cache-pin      off        [derived] not even one token cycle of the cache can be locked in place here after what is ahead of it, so the cache is ordinary memory and no lock is asked for
plan:   memory-ledger  15796 MiB  [derived] held 15796 of 24132 MiB holdable, of which locked 0 MiB of 0 MiB lockable: context and compute 110, expert cache 15686
plan:   storage-rate   2.5 GiB/s  [derived] this model's expert slice is 450 KiB and at that request size this storage gives 2531 MiB/s over 4 lanes: the expert lane is priced there, not at the storage's peak
plan:   storage-spread 1.7x       [derived] that rate varied by 1.7x across the probe's own repeats, so every predicted cost below carries at least that much uncertainty: treat the ranking as firmer than the totals
plan:   cache-floor    632 MiB    [derived] the engine's fixed cache guard (1500 MiB) is above this model's token cycle (632 MiB), so a budget between the two returns hits and needs force-cache to be accepted; the floor enforced here is the model's
plan:   device-unapplied -          [derived] a device won at least one group, but this build computes on the host: the placement is reported, not applied
plan:   cache-hits     94%        [measured] measured on this model: an LRU replay of the routing it recorded on an earlier run (256 decoded and 38 prompt tokens) at every cache size up to the whole expert set; at 15686 MiB it serves 94% of the decode lookups, where the linear credit would have said 48%
plan:   moe-stream     on         [derived] the experts do not fit but the dense set does
plan:   cache-mb       15686      [derived] what the cache is worth out of 24132 MiB usable, ranked against the resident groups by seconds saved per byte of RAM rather than handed the remainder: 18895 MiB committed in total, of which 15686 MiB is cache, over a floor of 632 MiB
plan:   io-threads     4          [measured] the highest measured rate for this model's 450 KiB expert slice on this storage
plan:   o-direct       on         [measured] uncached reads return correct bytes on this path
plan:   release-mmap   on         [measured] a live mapping of the model serialises this storage's concurrent uncached reads (1606 vs 6626 MiB/s), and this model's shape keeps no pointer that would survive the release (the session checks again before doing it)
plan:   lossy          off        [policy] no quality budget was given, and a policy that changes the output must never arm itself
plan:   overlap        off        [unprobed] hiding compute behind the reads changes no output and has measured faster, but one machine is not a rule and nothing here predicts where it stops paying: pass --overlap
